>> B2: Explain the data structure used to track priority donation.
>> Use ASCII art to diagram a nested donation.  (Alternately, submit a
>> .png file.)

Each thread will keep its base priority separately from its effective
priority. held_locks identifies the locks from which it can receive
donations. Each lock's contenders list holds threads whose acquisition
attempts have not yet succeeded, linked through lock_wait_elem.
waiting_lock connects a waiting thread to the next lock in the chain.

A contender will remain registered after it is unblocked and until it
actually obtains the lock. The semaphore wait list alone cannot represent
that interval. A new holder will therefore see donations from every
remaining acquisition attempt.

For H, M, and L with base priorities 50, 30, and 10:

    H (base 50)           M (base 30)           L (base 10)
    waits for A -------> holds A
                         waits for B -------> holds B
    effective 50         effective 50         effective 50

The effective priority is the maximum of the base priority and the
current effective priorities of contenders for all held locks. Donation
will follow waiting_lock and holder links through at most eight holders.
A null holder or repeated thread stops the traversal.

---- ALGORITHMS ----

>> B3: How do you ensure that the highest priority thread waiting for
>> a lock, semaphore, or condition variable wakes up first?

We will append ready threads and semaphore waiters in arrival order.
When selecting a thread, we will scan the list using its current effective
priority and keep the first maximum. A strictly greater priority replaces
the candidate; an equal priority does not. Equal-priority threads therefore
keep FIFO order, and a yielding thread joins the ready list at the tail.

sema_up() will select the highest-priority blocked waiter, remove it from
the wait list, unblock it, and increment the semaphore value before any
ordinary context switch. The lock's semaphore uses the same selection.
After the surrounding operation finishes its metadata updates, a higher
priority ready thread will cause a yield. An interrupt handler will request
intr_yield_on_return() instead of yielding inside the handler.
thread_create() will also check preemption after making a new thread ready.
Idle will yield to any ready thread, even one with priority zero. Ordinary
thread_unblock() will keep its atomic contract; its callers will check
preemption once their metadata updates are complete.

cond_signal() will scan condition waiters through their saved thread
pointers and use the same current-priority rule. This also works when a
signal arrives before the waiter's sema_down(), while its private
semaphore still has an empty wait list. sema_up() signals that waiter's
private semaphore; a blocked waiter becomes ready, while an early signal
is retained until its next sema_down(). The waiter must reacquire the monitor lock before
cond_wait() returns.

>> B4: Describe the sequence of events when a call to lock_acquire()
>> causes a priority donation.  How is nested donation handled?

If the lock is unavailable, lock_acquire() will set the caller's
waiting_lock and link its acquisition attempt into the lock's contenders.
It will recompute the holder's effective priority from the holder's base
priority and all contenders for its held locks.

If the holder is waiting for another lock, the recomputation will continue
to that lock's holder. This propagates a high-priority donation through a
nested chain, up to the eight-holder limit. The caller will then wait on
the lock semaphore. These waiting steps use sema_down() and thread_block(),
not a loop that repeatedly yields.

After the caller successfully takes the semaphore, it will remove its
contender entry if it registered one and clear waiting_lock. It will
assign the new holder and add the lock to held_locks, including on an
uncontended acquisition. It will recompute its effective priority from
any remaining contenders. A successful lock_try_acquire() will use the
same ownership bookkeeping; a failed try will not register or donate. The registration, ownership, and priority changes
will use the existing synchronization primitives' short atomic sections,
with blocking delegated to the semaphore.

>> B5: Describe the sequence of events when lock_release() is called
>> on a lock that a higher-priority thread is waiting for.

lock_release() will remove the lock from the current thread's held_locks
and clear the lock's holder. It will recompute the releasing thread's
effective priority using its base priority and contenders for its remaining
locks. This removes donations for this lock while preserving others.

It will then release the semaphore, selecting its highest-priority blocked
waiter. Contender records remain until their threads actually acquire the
lock. A new holder will inherit donations from remaining contenders,
including a contender already made ready but not yet successful.

All release bookkeeping and the semaphore increment will finish before a
normal-context preemption check. If a ready thread has a higher priority,
the releasing thread will yield immediately. Releasing a lock makes its
waiter ready; dispatch still chooses the highest-priority ready thread,
which can be another thread.

---- SYNCHRONIZATION ----

>> B6: Describe a potential race in thread_set_priority() and explain
>> how your implementation avoids it.  Can you use a lock to avoid
>> this race?

A race could occur if thread_set_priority() reads the old donation state,
then is preempted before storing the effective priority. Another thread
could add a higher donation while the setter is suspended. Writing the old
calculated value after resuming would discard that donation.

We will protect the base-priority change, donation scan, effective-priority
write, and ready-priority comparison in one short atomic update. The setter
will change base_priority and retain any larger active donation. Any yield
will happen after the metadata update is complete. thread_get_priority()
will return the effective value.

An extra sleeping lock is unsuitable for this scheduler update. Acquiring
it could block and introduce a donation dependency into the mechanism
that maintains donations, and interrupt handlers cannot acquire it.
Short interrupt exclusion protects this internal state on Pintos's single
CPU. Semaphores and locks still provide the actual blocking and waiting.

---- RATIONALE ----

>> B7: Why did you choose this design?  In what ways is it superior to
>> another design you considered?

Grouping donation sources by held lock makes releasing one lock clear:
its donations disappear while the other locks still contribute. Keeping
an acquisition in contenders until success also covers the gap between
wakeup and ownership, without allocating a separate donation object.

Selecting from current priorities avoids keeping every ready and waiting
list sorted after each donation. A sorted-list alternative could reduce
selection cost, but would need to update a thread's position whenever its
effective priority changes, including while blocked on another semaphore.

The cost of our plan is linear selection and scans of held locks and their
contenders. It also adds list links to threads and locks. This is a simple
starting point for the project; priority buckets or cached lock maxima
could reduce those scans later, at the cost of more update paths.

