>> C4: How is the way you divided the cost of scheduling between code
>> inside and outside interrupt context likely to affect performance?

Each timer tick will charge one recent_cpu unit to the running non-idle
thread. Alarm wakeups will follow. At a one-second boundary, load_avg will
be updated first, followed by recent_cpu for every non-idle thread,
including blocked threads. At each global four-tick boundary, all non-idle
priorities will be recalculated. A tick that matches both intervals will
use the new load_avg and recent_cpu in its priority calculation.

The calculations will use:

    priority = PRI_MAX - recent_cpu / 4 - 2 * nice
    load_avg = (59/60) * load_avg + (1/60) * ready_threads
    recent_cpu = (2*load_avg)/(2*load_avg+1) * recent_cpu + nice

The ready-thread count will be taken after alarm wakeups, so it includes
threads whose deadlines have arrived at that boundary, plus the running
non-idle thread. All these operations will finish before an interrupt
return can schedule an ordinary thread. The current thread_tick() and
timer handler will be separated into accounting and periodic updates to
produce this order.

The per-thread periodic scans cost O(n) for n threads. Ready selection
costs O(r) for r ready threads, and the alarm adds O(k+1) for k due
requests. Keeping all-thread scans at the specified boundaries avoids
doing them every tick, but those boundary interrupts are still longer.
Thread creation and thread_set_nice() will calculate the affected thread's
priority outside interrupt context; their shared-state changes will use
short atomic sections.

---- RATIONALE ----

>> C5: Briefly critique your design, pointing out advantages and
>> disadvantages in your design choices.  If you were to have extra
>> time to work on this part of the project, how might you choose to
>> refine or improve your design?

Reusing the ready list gives both scheduling policies the same selection
rule. We will read priorities at dispatch rather than rely on a list that
was sorted before the last periodic update. This also lets blocked threads
receive updated recent_cpu and priority without moving between queues.

The tradeoff is O(r) dispatch and O(n) periodic scans. Sixty-four FIFO ready
queues could make dispatch cheaper, but changed priorities would require
moving ready threads while preserving the chosen tie order. If the thread
count made scans expensive, that would be the first optimization to
consider. The simple list is our initial design.

Initialization will set the first thread's nice and recent_cpu to zero.
Later ordinary threads will inherit both values before they are published
in all_list. Idle will be recognized before it becomes ready and excluded
from these calculations. In MLFQS mode, the explicit creation priority and
thread_set_priority() will be ignored, and donation will be bypassed.
thread_set_nice() will recalculate immediately and check preemption.

After implementation, the nine MLFQS tests will check load, fairness,
niceness, recent CPU accounting, and blocked-thread behavior. Additional
review will cover idle startup, inherited state, negative arithmetic,
and ticks matching both update boundaries.

>> C6: The assignment explains arithmetic for fixed-point math in
>> detail, but it leaves it open to you to implement it.  Why did you
>> decide to implement it the way you did?  If you created an
>> abstraction layer for fixed-point math, that is, an abstract data
>> type and/or a set of functions or macros to manipulate fixed-point
>> numbers, why did you do so?  If not, why not?

We will use a signed 17.14 fixed-point type with scale F = 1 << 14.
Named helpers in threads/fixed-point.h will handle conversion and
arithmetic, keeping scale corrections out of the scheduler formulas.
A header of small inline functions is sufficient; no separate source
file or build-rule change is needed.

An integer n becomes n * F. Adding an integer uses x + n * F. Two
fixed-point values multiply as ((int64_t) x * y) / F and divide as
((int64_t) x * F) / y. The wide intermediate avoids overflow during
scaling. Multiplication and division by F will be used instead of signed
bit shifts, whose behavior is awkward for negative operands.

We will keep stored fractions intact, including a negative recent_cpu.
The priority formula will be evaluated in fixed point, then the complete
result converted toward zero and clamped to 0..63. For a valid positive
priority, this conversion rounds down. The recent_cpu/4 term will not be
converted early. The coefficient for the one-second recent_cpu update
will be calculated before multiplying by recent_cpu.

The recent CPU and load getters will multiply by 100 using a wide
intermediate, then round to nearest. For scaled x, that conversion uses
(x + F/2)/F when nonnegative and (x - F/2)/F when negative, with integer
division toward zero. These distinct helpers will make truncation and
rounding explicit at their call sites.

